On Mon, 10 May 2004 08:28:35 +0200, Svein Ove Aas wrote:

> Neither, actually. Functions and closures are called functions and
> closures. Macros are functions that are (generally) evaluated at
> compile-time, and which return code.

For "return code" as "are replaced by code", correct?  I'm not familiar
with lisp, so I'd like to have my facts about it relatively straight.

> As such, when the compiler runs a macro in Lisp, it can go be an
> arbitrarily complex transformation; you have the entire language
> available. You can even do I/O, although that's very much not
> recommended.
> 
> In comparison, C(++) has simple parametrized #defines at best.

C does.  C++ has some more advanced features that aren't related to
template metaprogramming, including inline functions, which seem to
satisfy the requirements of your definition of "macro".

> Here's a challenge:
> 
> #define use_twice(x) {foobar1(x); foobar2(x)}
> 
> Fairly obvious what this does, isn't it? So what happens if x is
> something like 'i++'?

'i' gets incremented twice.  We've been over this.  This is, in fact, a
very good reason not to use preprocessor macros.

> In Lisp you can solve it by having the macro expand into something that
> binds the value of x to a temporary value and uses that, evaluating x
> exactly once. What do you do in C(++)?

inline
void use_twice (int x) { /* int could be another type, depending on what
                            fb1 and fb2 expect */
  foobar1 (x); foobar2 (x);
}

which obeys function call semantics (calling it with an expression results
in a single exectution of the expression) but is intended to be replaced
with something functionally-identical to

...
{ /* new scope, so anything local to the function goes away as expected */
  int x = <argument>;
  foobar1 (x); foobar2 (x);
}
...

when compiled.

